  "query_preview": "..\n\nEfficient radiational cooling under mostly clear skies coupled with \npost-frontal northwest flow will allow temperatures to drop into the\nlow to mid 30s across most of SE MI over the next 1-3 hours, with \nlocalized colder temperatures expected within the northern Tri- \nCities. This will result in patchy to widespread frost development \nprior to daybreak. Frost and freeze headlines remain in effect until \n8 AM EDT. Below normal temperatures hold this afternoon as highs peak\nin the low to mid 50s under mostly sunny skies. Another round of \nclear skies overnight will produce another cool morning as lows \ndrop back into the low to mid 30s. Patchy to widespread frost will \nagain be possible but a slight uptick in gradient flow may help keep \ntemperatures a couple degrees warmer relative to this morning. \nTemperatures to then return to seasonal normals in the low 60s by \ntomorrow afternoon as flow will have backed to the southwest in \nresponse to high pressure building in through the Ohio Valley and \nAppalachia region. \n\nHigh pressure under ridge amplification will then bring an extended \nperiod of dry weather and above normal temperatures for the \nremainder of the forecast period through the end of the week. This\npersistence forecast will see mostly sunny conditions and highs \nin the mid to upper 60s. Some readings in the low 70s will be \npossible towards the middle to end of the week once the ridge \namplifies across the Midwest in response to a closed low that will \ntravel south along the coastal Pacific, turning inland across \nsouthern California and opening up across the Four Corners region.",
